  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000122649,
      "SummaryText": "Sunoco LLC is located in Birmingham, Alabama. The facility is a TransMix processing facility. Operation of the Risk Management Plan (RMP) covered processes involves the use or handling of certain listed regulated substances as defined by the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) in 40 CFR Part 68. Currently, the regulated substances at the site are anhydrous ammonia and hydrogen.\n\nAnhydrous Ammonia is stored in a pressurized bullet tank with a maximum rated capacity of 2,000 gallons (10,300 lbs). An administrative control will be placed on the tank restricting it to an 80 percent fill (1,600 gallons or 8,240 lbs).\n\nHydrogen is stored in a pressurized bullet tank with a maximum rated capacity of 20,000 gallons (11,800 lbs). An administrative control will be placed on the tank restricting it to a 60 percent fill (12,000 gallons or 7,080 lbs).  \n\nThe facility coordinates as measured by the front gate entrance are latitude 33.458671 and longitude -86.878189 decimal degrees; the facility coordinates as measured at the anhydrous ammonia tank are latitude 33.458444 and longitude -86.876772; and the facility coordinates as measured at the hydrogen tank are latitude 33.458533 and longitude -86.877051.\n\nThe facility accidental release prevention and response practices and programs are designed to protect the employees, contractors, visitors, the general public and the environment. \n\nA hazard assessment was performed to analyze releases for potential impacts to the surrounding community and environment. These assessments consist of determining \"worst case\" and \"alternative case\" scenarios as specified by the EPA. Since the facility is a Program 3 source, and because of the site lay out, one \"worst case\" and one \"alternative case\" scenario have been selected. \n\nCompany employees are encouraged to actively participate in accident prevention at Sunoco, LLC. Employee participation in these efforts is accomplished on several levels both formally and informally. \n\nAt Sunoco, many wr"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000122649,
      "SummaryText": "itten documents are available which provide guidance on how to safely operate the various processes within the plant. The documents related to process safety information are utilized for training of employees and contractors to identify and understand the hazards associated with each process. The documents are also utilized as a resource when conducting process hazards analyses and are utilized as a reference during unit operations. \n\nSunoco utilizes PHAs to ensure controls are in place to minimize, manage and control hazards that may potentially affect both on-site and off-site personnel and the environment. In addition to performing PHAs that are triggered by changes, PHAs are also performed to revalidate unit PHAs at least every five years. \n\nSunoco has a fully developed set of Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) for the covered processes within the plant. These operating procedures have been developed to be consistent with process safety information and are maintained as such through annual reviews by operating personnel. \n\nSunoco has a comprehensive training program in place for employees involved in operating the plant. This program is designed to address the needs of new employees and experienced operators. Sunoco utilizes both classroom and on the job training. \n\nAll PSM/RMP affected employees are properly trained in all areas of handling highly hazardous materials and in the processes that use such materials. \n\nContractors performing maintenance or repair, turnaround, major renovation, or specialized work on or adjacent to a covered process must meet certain training and safety performance requirements as described in Sunoco's Process Safety Management Program procedure. \n\nAs required by OSHA and EPA regulations and in keeping with good operating practice, Sunoco conducts pre-startup safety reviews when a modification to a process is significant enough to change process safety information. \n\nSunoco has practices and procedures such as the Process Safety Ma"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 2,
      "FacilityID": 1000122649,
      "SummaryText": "nagement procedure in place to ensure that equipment has been designed, constructed, installed and maintained to contain the chemicals in the process. Any equipment or system used to store, process and handle highly hazardous chemicals is included in the mechanical integrity program. \n\nHot-work operations on or near a covered process can only begin after a Hot-work permit has been issued. The hot-work permit procedure includes a list of hazards that must be identified and details compliance with fire prevention and protection requirements. \n\nMOCs at the facility are created and maintained in the Gensuite Benchmark online platform. The program provides a system to thoroughly evaluate a process change as it relates to chemicals handled or processed, design or technology, equipment use or maintenance, procedures for operation and changes to ancillary facilities affected by the process. \n\nA written incident investigation and reporting guideline (Process Safety Management) has been developed and implemented by Sunoco. Incidents are recorded in the Gensuite Benchmark system and tracked to closure.  This program formalizes the process by which an accident must be reported and investigated by employees (and contractors when appropriate) to determine the direct and/or contributing causes of an incident. \n\nThe plant has a comprehensive set of emergency procedures consisting of several plans which have been written to address the various types of anticipatable emergencies that might occur at the plant\n\nIt is the company's policy to conduct an audit at least every three years on the elements of OSHA's PSM standard and EPA's RMP regulation. Various employees including hourly and salaried employees participate in the audit by gathering required documentation, researching requested documentation, verifying information and participating in interviews. \n\nFuture plans to improve safety are developed through several venues including the Employee Safety Suggestion program, Monthly Safe"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 3,
      "FacilityID": 1000122649,
      "SummaryText": "ty Meetings, Process Hazard Analyses, and Job Safety Analysis. These safety action items are implemented through individual employees, employee committees or teams, and are supported by the site management team."
    }
  ]
}